<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1086">1086</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish was a Seljuk prince who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Taking advantage of Byzantine civil wars after Manzikert, he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> around 1075 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 1084, displacing local lords such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a>. He was killed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1086 fighting Tutush, brother of the Great Seljuk sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish.jpg" alt="Suleiman ibn Qutalmish" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CeeGee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MonumentOfKutalmi%C5%9Fo%C4%9FluS%C3%BCleyman%C5%9EahTarsus.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Suleiman was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutalmish">Qutalmish</a>, who struggled unsuccessfully against his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> for the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuk Empire</a>. When Qutalmish died in 1064, Suleiman fled with his three brothers into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a> and took refuge with Turkoman tribes living beyond the empire's borders. Alp Arslan answered with a series of punitive expeditions. Of the four brothers only Suleiman and his brother Mansur survived the raids, and Suleiman consolidated his leadership of the Turkomans of the Taurus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Suleiman founded an independent Seljuk state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and ruled as Seljuk sultan of Rum until his death in 1086. The chronicler al-Azimi says he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in 1075. The Great Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> recognised him as ruler of Rum, and the Abbasid caliph al-Qa'im sent him an edict and a robe of honour. No record shows him minting coins or having the sermon delivered in his own name, and several sources state his subjection to Malik-Shah, so he was probably still subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuk Empire</a>.</p><p>In 1078 the Byzantine emperor Michael VII asked for his help against Nicephorus Botaneiates, who had challenged him for the throne. Botaneiates persuaded Suleiman and Mansur to join his rebellion with better offers, and after his success their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> were allowed to settle on the Asiatic side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Two years later Suleiman backed another pretender, Nicephorus Melissenus, who opened the gates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> to the Turkmen. All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> soon fell to Suleiman. In 1084 he captured Antakya from the Byzantine governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a>. The Uqaylid Muslim ibn Quraysh demanded tribute, Suleiman refused, and in 1085 he defeated Muslim's army after Turkmen under Cubuk bey defected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1086 Suleiman besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Its emir offered the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush I</a>, the Seljuk ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and Suleiman lifted the siege to meet him. At the battle of Ain Salm near Aleppo, his forces fled before Tutush's army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuq-ibn-ekseb">Artuk Bey</a>, and Suleiman was killed. Malik-Shah marched to Antakya, where Suleiman's vizier surrendered the city and Suleiman's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-i">Kilij Arslan I</a>, whom Malik-Shah took to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> as a hostage. After Malik-Shah's death Kilij Arslan re-established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suleiman ibn Qutalmish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, <b>Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</b>, was a member of the Seljuk family whose father Qutalmish had died rebelling against Sultan Alp Arslan in 1064.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seljuk prince <b>Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</b> took Antioch in 1084, and Edessa fell soon after.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-philaretos-brachamios">Principality of Philaretos Brachamios</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1086 he defeated and killed the Rum Seljuk <b>Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</b> near Aleppo and extended his rule over most of Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-syria">Seljuk Sultanate of Syria</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslim ibn Quraysh was killed in 1085 fighting <b>Sulayman ibn Qutalmish</b> of the Sultanate of Rum.</blockquote><a href="/state/uqaylid-dynasty">Uqaylid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Seljuk dynasty. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=1076" aria-label="Antioch on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Suleiman ibn Qutalmish"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5424 4459h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5424 4459h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,36.15&amp;year=1076">What was here in 1076? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
